-- title:
--   j(q^N) and j are mutually integral
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $\ell$ a prime. Write $F$ for `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subset \mathbb{Q}((q))$ (Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$) obtained by adjoining to $\mathbb{Q}$ the set of all series $q \mapsto j(q^{d})$ for nonzero divisors $d \mid N$, i.e. the images `qExpand ℚ d jq` of the $q$-expansion `jq` under the substitution $q \mapsto q^{d}$. Two elements of $F$ are in play: `jFull N`, namely `jq` itself, and the element determined by `jqN N` $=$ `qExpand ℚ N jq`, which lies in $F$ because it is one of these divisor expansions. For a subset $S \subseteq F$, `chartAlg N ℓ S` is the subalgebra of $F$, over the coefficient ring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt ℓ`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8), consisting of the elements of $F$ integral over the subalgebra generated by $S$ over that ring; `chartAlgFin N ℓ` is the case $S = \{$`jFull N`$\}$. The theorem asserts the conjunction: `jqN N` is integral over the algebra generated by `jFull N`, and `jFull N` is integral over the algebra generated by `jqN N`.
--
--   This is the integrality half of Kronecker's modular equation in the form needed for the Igusa model of $X_0(N)$: the two generators $j$ and $j(q^N)$ of the level-$N$ modular function field are integral over one another after localisation at $\ell$, so the corresponding chart algebras are finite over each other. It is used in the construction and comparison of the affine charts of the Igusa scheme and in the subsequent identification of their tensor products and of Frobenius on points.
-- source:
